# Zero-Shield CLI - AWS CloudShell Environment Configuration
# 
# This configuration is optimized for AWS CloudShell deployment where
# AWS credentials are automatically inherited from your CloudShell session.
#
# SETUP INSTRUCTIONS:
# 1. Copy this file: cp .env.example .env
# 2. Edit the new .env file: nano .env
# 3. Replace the placeholder values:
#    - Replace "your_github_token_here" with your actual GitHub token
#    - Replace "sg-your_quarantine_group_id_here" with your actual security group ID
# 4. Save and close the file
# 5. Run: python3 zero_shield_cli.py

# =============================================================================
# REQUIRED CONFIGURATION
# =============================================================================

# GitHub Models API Token (REQUIRED)
# This token is for accessing GitHub's AI Models API (GPT-4o, Llama, etc.)
# This is NOT for repository access - it's for calling AI models hosted by GitHub
# Get this from: https://github.com/settings/tokens
# Required scopes: NONE (create a token with no checkboxes selected)
# The token allows Zero-Shield to call GitHub's LLM inference API
GITHUB_TOKEN=your_github_token_here

# =============================================================================
# OPTIONAL CONFIGURATION
# =============================================================================

# Quarantine Security Group ID (RECOMMENDED)
# Create a restrictive security group for quarantining compromised instances
# Example: aws ec2 create-security-group --group-name ZeroShield-Quarantine --description "Quarantine zone"
QUARANTINE_SG_ID=sg-your_quarantine_group_id_here

# =============================================================================
# AWS CREDENTIALS (NOT NEEDED FOR CLOUDSHELL)
# =============================================================================
# 
# AWS CloudShell automatically inherits your AWS console session credentials.
# These variables are NOT needed and should be left commented out:
#
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=not_needed_in_cloudshell
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=not_needed_in_cloudshell
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=inherited_from_cloudshell_session

# =============================================================================
# ADVANCED CONFIGURATION (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================

# GitHub Models API Base URL (Optional - for custom endpoints)
# Default: https://models.inference.ai.azure.com
# GITHUB_MODELS_URL=https://models.inference.ai.azure.com

# Session Encryption Key Override (Optional)
# By default, GITHUB_TOKEN is used as the encryption key for session files
# Uncomment to use a different key:
# SESSION_ENCRYPTION_KEY=your_custom_32_character_key_here

# Debug Mode (Optional)
# Uncomment to enable verbose logging:
# DEBUG=true

# =============================================================================
# CLOUDSHELL-SPECIFIC NOTES
# =============================================================================
#
# 1. AWS Credentials: Automatically inherited from your CloudShell session
#    - No need to configure AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID or AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
#    - Region is inherited from your CloudShell session
#    - IAM permissions come from your AWS console user/role
#
# 2. File Persistence: CloudShell has limited persistent storage
#    - Session files (session_state.json, session_kg.json) will persist
#    - Files are automatically encrypted using your GITHUB_TOKEN
#
# 3. Network Access: CloudShell has internet access for GitHub Models API
#    - No firewall configuration needed
#    - GitHub Models API calls work out of the box
#
# 4. Security: CloudShell provides additional security layers
#    - Temporary environment that resets after inactivity
#    - Inherits your AWS console security settings
#    - No long-term credential storage needed
#
# =============================================================================
# QUICK SETUP COMMANDS
# =============================================================================
#
# 1. Copy this template:
#    cp .env.example .env
#
# 2. Edit with your values:
#    nano .env
#
# 3. Test configuration:
#    python3 -c "import os; from dotenv import load_dotenv; load_dotenv(); print('✓ GITHUB_TOKEN:', 'SET' if os.getenv('GITHUB_TOKEN') else 'NOT SET')"
#
# 4. Run Zero-Shield:
#    python3 zero_shield_cli.py
#